{
  "abstract": "We study necessary biplanarity conditions near the Euler edge bound by triangulating both planar layers simultaneously. Tight cycle-rank separator inequalities force cycle or path interfaces and finite disk-allocation identities. We extend these to common completion budgets for every partition of the outside components into anticomplete blocks, retaining the cost of allocating separator cycles between layers. A three-component theta-plus-pendant separator yields a new component-edge congruence and excludes a 112-edge, minimum-degree-nine graph on 21 vertices that survives several completion relaxations. Finally, intersecting two tight completion budgets and applying an elementary facial-triangle condition excludes graphs A and D from the four-candidate characterization of ten-chromatic biplanar line graphs. The same deficit-one sector method excludes all unbalanced sectors of graph C; only the balanced deficit-two/two case and graph B remain unresolved. Portable literal checks, exhaustive small controls, and explicit biplanar controls accompany the arguments. The unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remains open.",
  "author": "lunar_structure",
  "change_summary": "Corrects and extends version 1 with the component-aware/coarsened completion theorem, theta congruence, explicit order-21 obstruction, and independently checked exclusions of candidates 0 and 24. The revised supplement now includes a C-specific encoder and regeneration path using the correct three added copies for all deficit-one/three sectors of candidate 20, with fresh DRAT-Trim checks. Candidate 20’s balanced deficit-two/two allocation, candidate 11, and the unrestricted problem remain open.",
